    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "What this dashboard cannot see",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 7,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 6
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "- **Suricata watches the Skids (VLAN 20) interface only**, alert-only (`Block Offenders` is off). Guest/VLAN 10 is next — [#89](https://github.com/Gerrrt/HomeLab/issues/89) — the rest of the lab is unwatched and WAN deliberately always will be. Empty Suricata panels mean *nothing was seen on VLAN 20*, not that the lab is quiet.\n- **A quiet IDS and a stopped one produce identical output**, and no panel here can distinguish them. `SuricataStopped` can: it reads the firewall's process table over SNMP and fires per declared interface, and it is listed in the alert table on this dashboard ([#90](https://github.com/Gerrrt/HomeLab/issues/90)). Empty Suricata panels with that alert quiet mean the sensor is running and saw nothing.\n- **Addresses are parsed out of the line at query time, IPv4 only.** `src` and `dst` are not labels — ADR-0003 keeps them out of the index — so the address panels use `| regexp` and skip IPv6 lines.\n- **Passed packets are logged only by the tripwire rules.** Three `pass`+`log` rules on `igc0.10`, `igc0.20` and `igc0.40` match terminal→internal traffic and sit *below* the block rules that stop it, so they log nothing while segmentation holds and add no volume. A line appearing there means the blocks were removed or reordered — ADR-0002 broken — and `TerminalSegmentReachedInternalNetwork` fires on it ([#223](https://github.com/Gerrrt/HomeLab/issues/223)). Ordinary egress is not logged, which is why *Passed (1h)* reads *not logged* rather than counting traffic.\n- **`Interface` is the pfSense device name**, not the VLAN name. On this firewall the VLAN sub-interfaces are `igc0.<vlan id>` — `igc0.20` is Skids, `igc0.10` is Degens — and `em0` is WAN. That mapping is a property of the current interface assignment, not a guarantee; group by the label rather than hardcoding a value. The label has two provenances: filterlog's is parsed from the line, Suricata's is *asserted* from each interface's syslog facility (`local1` → `igc0.20`, `local2` → `igc0.10`, mapped in `syslog.alloy`), because the alert line does not say which interface saw it. A Suricata interface on an unmapped facility carries no `interface` label at all, and because every Suricata panel here filters on `$interface` (whose *All* is `.+`), its alerts do not appear on this dashboard — hidden, not mislabelled. The unfiltered `sum by (interface) (count_over_time({app=\"suricata\"}[10m]))` in runbook §6 is what shows it, as a series with an empty `interface`."
      }
    },
